   "subject": "Re: Adding a number of lines to a boxplot / sorting (again)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Here is a simplified version on a manufactured (and reproducible) toy data frame:<div><br></div><div><div>DF &lt;- data.frame(g = factor(rep(seq(3), each = 50)),</div><div>                 y = rnorm(150, mean = rep(c(5, 10, 15), each = 50)))</div><div><br></div><div>DF2 &lt;- data.frame(trt = paste0(&quot;treat&quot;, 1:3),</div><div>                  val = c(3, 9, 16))</div><div><br></div><div>library(ggplot2)</div><div><br></div><div>ggplot() +</div><div>   geom_boxplot(data = DF, aes(x = reorder(g, -y, median), y = y)) +</div><div>   geom_hline(data = DF2, aes(yintercept = val, color = trt), </div><div>                        size = 1, show_guide = TRUE) +</div><div>   scale_color_manual(values = c(&quot;blue&quot;, &quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot;)) +</div><div>   labs(x = &quot;&quot;, color = &quot;treat&quot;)</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Notes:</div><div><br></div><div>1. Since the aesthetics for geom_boxplot() and geom_hline() differ, it is safer to map the aesthetics locally in each geom. Since the data frames differ in each geom, one can also</div><div>define the data frames for each plot layer locally.</div><div>2. Notice how the reorder() function [in geom_boxplot()] produces descending order by ordering -y rather than y. This is necessary because reorder() does not have a decreasing = argument.</div><div>3. By default, geom_hline() does not produce a legend guide. You need to add show_guide = TRUE to the call to get one.</div><div>4. It is your responsibility to provide a minimal reproducible example; fortunately, this was a straightforward problem and at least you provided a graph as a template of what you wanted.</div><div><br></div><div>Dennis</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, Dec 9, 2014 at 1:05 AM, trichter <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tric...@uni-bremen.de</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Hi!<br><br>I want to boxplot vectors in a list, ordered them by median and add vertical(horizontal) lines to the plot:<br><br>list1 &lt;- lapply(dir(), read.table,  sep = &quot;\\t&quot;, dec = &quot;.&quot;)<br>names(list1) &lt;- gsub(&quot;.tsv&quot;, &quot;&quot;, c(dir()))<br>vector1 &lt;- lapply(list1, function(x) {as.numeric(as.vector(unlist(x)))})<br>med &lt;- sapply(vector1, median)<br><br>old.par &lt;- par(no.readonly=TRUE)<br>par(las=2)<br>boxplot(vector1[order(med, decreasing=TRUE)])<br>abline(h=87.65, col=&quot;Red&quot;)<br>abline(h=83.55, col=&quot;Green&quot;)<br>abline(h=80.38, col=&quot;Blue&quot;)<br><br>The resulting plot looks as depicted in the bottom (first pic).<br><br>I want to do it in ggplot, mainly because i want to have a legend for the horizontal lines. I started with a melt:<br><br>v1m  &lt;- melt(vector1)<br><br>and then do<br><br>ggplot(data=v1m, aes(x=L1, y=value)) +<br>  stat_boxplot(geom =&#39;errorbar&#39;) + <br>  geom_boxplot()+<br>  theme_bw() +<br>  coord_flip()<br><br><b>The\r\n first question is: How to put the ordering by median in to action?</b> I \r\nknow this has been asked ad nauseam on the web, but there are always \r\nlittle twists to each individual problems.<br><br>Then its about adding those lines. I added: <br>geom_hline(aes(yintercept=87.8), colour=&quot;red&quot;, linetype = &quot;longdash&quot;) +, <br>, which was fine, but as the intercept are coming from a dataframe:</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:13px;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;w